Dear ImGui already contains much appreciated support for debuggers to visualize it's custom types at `misc/debuggers`.

This PR introduces the same capability for [LLDB](https://lldb.llvm.org/), the choice of debugger of Xcode and macOS. Here is a quick sample of a before/after:

```c++
ImVec2 vec2(1, 2);
ImVec4 vec4(1, 2, 3, 4);
ImRect rect(1, 2, 3, 4);

ImVector<int> vec;
vec.push_back(1);
vec.push_back(2);
vec.push_back(3);
vec.push_back(4);

ImSpan<int> span(vec.begin()+1, vec.begin()+3);

printf("Breakpoint here!");
```

Before | After |
--- | ---
<img width="371" height="179" alt="image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/f7c7b9c5-aba7-4816-998e-32750fa7cd21" /> | <img width="371" height="199" alt="image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/4334199b-609f-44de-b5d2-0f4ed0988a9d" />

Admittedly the amount of code to achieve this might be scary. LLDB's scripting integration was built with more complex data structures in mind, thus doing simple things unfortunately takes quite a bit of legwork.

Regardless I thought this might be useful for people who use either LLDB or Xcode to work with Dear ImGui.

Fixed an issue where the timer would briefly display negative values (like "-00:01") when reaching zero before triggering the completion alert.

## Problem
The timer interval logic had a race condition where it would:
1. Decrement `remainingSeconds` (potentially making it negative)
2. Update the display with the negative value
3. Check if the timer should complete

This caused users to see confusing negative time values for a brief moment before the "Time to flip!" alert appeared.

## Solution
Reordered the timer interval logic in the `startTimer()` method to:
1. Decrement `remainingSeconds`
2. **Immediately** check if it reaches zero or below
3. Set to exactly zero and trigger completion without displaying negatives
4. Only update display with non-negative values

```javascript
// Before: Could show negative values
this.remainingSeconds--;
this.updateDisplay(); // Could display "-00:01"
if (this.remainingSeconds <= 0) {
    this.timerComplete();
}

// After: Never shows negative values
this.remainingSeconds--;
if (this.remainingSeconds <= 0) {
    this.remainingSeconds = 0;
    this.updateDisplay(); // Always displays "00:00"
    this.timerComplete();
    return;
}
this.updateDisplay();
```

## Testing
Verified the fix with multiple timer durations:
- 30-second timer completes cleanly at "00:00" 
- 2-second timer completes cleanly at "00:00"
- Timer completion properly triggers the "Time to flip!" alarm
- All existing functionality preserved (pause, stop, add time, etc.)

The timer now provides a clean countdown experience that stops exactly at zero without confusing negative displays.

![Timer completing at exactly 00:00 with alarm](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/6d0082d4-783d-48aa-9fbf-389d098206f6)

